<% content_for :title, "How it works · Spike or Stay" %>
<%= render "shared/nav" %>

<main>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
      <h1>How Spike or Stay makes a call</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Given an abrupt surge in public interest, will it last? Here's every rule the app uses, how the model was
        tested, and what it can't do. The full write-up and code are in the project's README and
        <code>backtest/PREREGISTRATION.md</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h2>1. Interest data</h2>
      <p>Daily Wikipedia pageviews (English, all devices, human readers only) from July 2015, summed into Monday–Sunday weeks.
        Each product is matched to an article through its product type, falling back to its title.</p>

      <h2>2. When did a surge start?</h2>
      <div class="rule-card">
        A week is the <strong>onset</strong> if its views are <strong>more than 3× the median of the previous 26 weeks</strong>,
        at least <strong>2,000 views</strong>, and both hold for <strong>two weeks in a row</strong>.
      </div>
      <p>Articles created mid-trend have no history to compare against, so for them the onset is the first time they reach
        the 2,000/week floor for two weeks in a row. The two-week rule was added after one-day Main Page and news blips were
        found triggering onsets (logged as amendment 1).</p>

      <h2>3. Was it sustained? (the label)</h2>
      <div class="rule-card">
        <strong>Sustained</strong> if average weekly views in weeks 26–51 after onset are at least <strong>25% of the peak week</strong>
        in weeks 0–25. Otherwise, <strong>spike</strong>. No hand labelling.
      </div>

      <h2>4. What the model sees</h2>
      <p>Only the first six weeks after onset (weeks 0–5). Tests feed in fake data after week 5 and check nothing changes.</p>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Growth</td><td>Average of weeks 3–5 vs weeks 0–2 (log ratio)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Acceleration</td><td>Is growth speeding up or slowing down?</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Peak sharpness</td><td>Highest week ÷ average week</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Volatility</td><td>How much views jump week to week</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>A logistic regression on these four (standardized) features outputs the probability a surge is sustained.
        The Rails app applies the exported weights in plain Ruby, and a parity test checks it matches Python to 1e-9.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%# Computed from backtest/results/step_d_predictions.csv (BacktestStats); McNemar p from step_d_results.md. %>
  <section class="section section-soft" id="results">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Backtest</p>
      <h2>Results: n = <%= TrendModel.instance.trained_on["n"] %> past trends</h2>
      <p>32 candidate trends were listed and audited before any data was examined. 16 had a clear onset and a full year of follow-up.
        Leave-one-out cross-validation; brackets are 95% Wilson confidence intervals.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="data-table results">
          <thead><tr><th>Analysis</th><th>n</th><th>Majority</th><th>"Still rising"</th><th>Model</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <% [[:a, "Primary", true], [:b, "Excluding articles created mid-trend", false],
                [:c, "Robustness: 2× multiplier", true], [:d, "Original 1-week rule", true]].each do |key, name, show_ci| %>
              <tr class="<%= "is-primary" if key == :a %>">
                <td><%= name %></td><td><%= BacktestStats.n(key) %></td>
                <td><%= BacktestStats.percent(key, :majority) %></td>
                <td><%= BacktestStats.percent(key, :naive) %><% if key == :a %> <span>[<%= BacktestStats.interval(key, :naive).delete_suffix("%") %>]</span><% end %></td>
                <td><%= key == :a ? tag.strong(BacktestStats.percent(key, :logreg)) : BacktestStats.percent(key, :logreg) %>
                  <span><%= show_ci ? "[#{BacktestStats.interval(key, :logreg).delete_suffix('%')}]" : "too small to read" %></span></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callouts">
        <div class="callout"><strong>The good</strong><p>The model beats both baselines in all four analyses, and the learned weights make sense: sharp, jumpy early weeks point to a spike.</p></div>
        <div class="callout"><strong>The caveat</strong><p>Against "still rising in week 6" in the primary analysis, <% model_k, = BacktestStats.score(:a, :logreg) %><% naive_k, = BacktestStats.score(:a, :naive) %>the model wins by <%= model_k - naive_k %> trends (<%= model_k %> vs <%= naive_k %>). An exploratory McNemar test gives p = 0.69: we can't tell them apart yet.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">The majority baseline's 0% is a leave-one-out artifact on balanced data: removing a trend always makes its own class the minority. Chance is 50%.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="limitations">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Limitations</p>
      <h2>What it can't do (yet)</h2>
      <ul class="checks checks-muted">
        <li><strong>Small dataset.</strong> 16 labelled trends. Treat accuracy as a signal, not a guarantee.</li>
        <li><strong>Only abrupt surges.</strong> Gradual growth (keto, pickleball, matcha) never shows a 3× jump, so it reads "No surge detected".</li>
        <li><strong>Interest isn't demand.</strong> Readers aren't buyers, and brand articles dilute product interest.</li>
        <li><strong>Seasonality.</strong> 8 of 16 onsets fall in November or December; holiday peaks can inflate the label baseline.</li>
        <li><strong>Mapping mistakes.</strong> "The Minimal Snowboard" once matched a video game. A sanity check and one-click overrides catch most, not all.</li>
        <li><strong>No sales data yet.</strong> Order webhooks and sales velocity are the top next step.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <%= link_to "Open the dashboard", dashboard_path, class: "btn btn-primary" %>
        <%= link_to "Try the air fryer replay", dashboard_path(as_of: "2017-12-31"), class: "btn btn-outline" %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<%= render "shared/footer" %>
